<p class="copy">
	St-1/E1 forms the first half of the <?php echo sf_lore_link('fuel-gates', 'fuel gate'); ?>/water gate duopoly of technologies that underpin space travel and colonization in the settled systems. Named after its paraphysical effect, whereby the element experiences much-accelerated chemical synthesis in combinatorial reactions, Fast Hydrogen is a common sight in heavy manufacturing, chemical processing, and military applications. The best-known use of Fast Hydrogen, though, is, of course, as the fuel in the hydrogen/oxygen burn cycle of rocket propulsion, where its significant improvement in reactivity over mundane hydrogen allows for rockets with far greater rates of thrust and substantially increased specific impulse.
</p>
<p class="copy mb-0">
	Fast Hydrogen’s increased speed of reaction makes its handling substantially more dangerous than the already famously slippery behavior of its mundane variety. The element can react with oxygen at temperatures as low as 32°C, resulting in uncontrolled burn-offs at even the slightest leak, and that’s to say nothing of what the element does to a living body unlucky enough to come into direct contact with it. Severe burns on the skin, flash fires within the respiratory tract, and even micro-explosions within the bloodstream have been recorded in instances where the element has mixed in trace amounts with the cold oxygen atmosphere of a ship. It’s perhaps fortunate, then, that Fast Hydrogen has a paraphysical decay half-life of just over 18 hours, safely transmuting into mundane hydrogen with no ill effects.
</p>
